RECON — automated improvement log
=================================
One entry per scheduled run: what changed, and what's worth picking up next.


2026-07-24 — Empty states, loading skeletons, responsive shell, line-ending fix
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First automated run, so it started by clearing two things that would have
taxed every run after it.

Housekeeping first (two separate commits, kept apart from the feature work):
  * Checkpointed pre-existing uncommitted work found in the tree — Backup
    promoted from a modal to a full rail view, tray master/client discovery,
    Chrome/Edge app-mode window — so there was a rollback point before
    touching anything.
  * Added .gitattributes and normalized static/ to LF. The four static files
    were saved CRLF against LF-stored blobs, so each one diffed as a total
    rewrite: app.js showed ~5,900 changed lines for ~115 lines of real edits.
    This run's feature diff is 368 lines and actually reviewable as a result.
    Also un-ignored auto_improvements.log, which the blanket *.log rule in
    .gitignore was silently swallowing.

Bug fixed:
  * wireVehiclesView() bound its filter chips with the global selector
    ".filters .chip", which matched every chip in the app — the A/P date
    ranges, cores, returns, and task filters. Clicking any of those also ran
    the vehicles handler, wiping the active state off every chip on every
    screen and setting state.filter to undefined. Now scoped to
    "#view-vehicles .filters .chip", matching how every other view already
    did it.

UI work:
  * Empty states: one emptyState()/emptyRow() component replaced ~14 hand-
    rolled inline-styled one-liners. Each list now distinguishes "nothing
    added yet" (with a button that starts the thing) from "your search or
    filter hid it all" (with a way to clear it) instead of a flat "No
    vehicles match." Covers vehicles, A/P, cores, returns, staff, backups,
    tasks, suggestions, vendors, notes, activity, deposits, estimate lines,
    and report output. Failed loads render in the critical colour so they
    aren't mistaken for an ordinary empty list.
  * Loading skeletons: showView() now paints shimmer rows before the fetch
    starts. Previously the old view's rows sat on screen until the request
    resolved, so the app looked like it had ignored the click.
  * Responsive: the shell was a fixed 236px rail and a hardcoded 4-column
    stats grid. Added breakpoints — stats reflow at 1240/700, the rail
    narrows at 1100 and collapses to an icon strip at 900, filters and topbar
    wrap. Matters more than it looks like it does now that the tray opens the
    UI in a resizable app-mode window.
  * Focus rings via :focus-visible (buttons, chips, rail items and rows had
    none — keyboard nav was invisible), themed scrollbars, and the topbar
    shows the shop name again via the .shop-name rule that had been sitting
    in the stylesheet unused. Dropped the dead .shop-acronym rule.

Tests: added tests/test_static_assets.py (5 tests) — cross-checks that every
$("#id") in app.js resolves to an element in index.html (259 references
checked), that rail buttons point at views that exist, that referenced
/assets/ files are present, that no id is declared twice, and that new empty
states go through the shared component. There's no build step for this
front-end, so nothing else catches a renamed element; a dead selector just
silently returns null. All five were mutation-tested to confirm they fail
when the thing they check is broken.

Suite: 155 -> 160 passing. Server boots clean; /, /assets/*, /api/health and
/api/dashboard all verified over real HTTP.

Next time, in rough priority order:
  1. The vehicle detail page is the densest screen and still the least
     polished — the parts/labor grid is an 11-column CSS grid with hardcoded
     fr units that will overflow badly under ~1100px, where the rest of the
     app now reflows. It needs a real responsive treatment or a horizontal
     scroll container.
  2. renderEstimate() and friends re-bind every row's listeners on every
     render. Event delegation (as now used for the empty-state buttons)
     would cut a lot of that and fix the subtle focus-loss on re-render.
  3. No client-side error boundary: a throw inside a render function leaves
     a half-drawn screen with no message. A try/catch at the view level that
     renders the error empty-state variant would make failures legible.
  4. Toasts are the only feedback channel and they auto-dismiss at 3.2s —
     destructive actions (void, delete, restore) deserve a confirm pattern
     better than window.confirm().
  5. Consider a real front-end smoke test (Playwright) — the static checks
     added this run catch dead selectors but nothing exercises a click path.
